text:
Soon Aluda cast his eye on the Kists’ trail.  
He shifted swiftly; the gun went off with a crack:  
one thieving Ghilghveli had a bad moment;  
he falls from the horse, hanging head and neck downward;  
the bullet wound is at the shoulder blade, and fire seems to cling to it;  
the slain Ghilghveli, his companion, is carrying the flintlock.  
Mutsal’s gun burst forth, the cliff face would split apart,  
and the lead, dug out by the shot, would fall into Aluda’s skirt.

II. The sun rose into the sky, the mists descended into the gorges;  
the giants of the Caucasus have gathered at the edge of the sky.  
The hawks have set out for the hunt, the swift destroyers of birds;  
the falcons are chasing the eagles, their companions in eating for free.

I could not bring myself to cut off my right hand;  
my heart grew angry with me: “Do not do what is to be done with difficulty:  
‘Let the name suffer loss; I am preferable to you as a counselor.’”

III. It grew dark, the waters are weeping, the hem of night burns us;  
It is the time of the stars’ twinkling, of dew falling upon the grass,  
of the dead souls rising from the grave, the time of their quiet song.

Khetelauri grew furious, taking on the color of a wolf;  
he drew his French sword, and the light burst forth like the sun.  
He struck the bull on the neck, and its head rolled down below.  
Then he pleaded with his lord: “Do not spare my son for me;  
let his belly be given to ruin, that unbaptized hero.”

At hearing this, Mindia’s eyes welled with tears;  
he can no longer help Aluda.  
He crosses his arms over his chest in grief;  
the sons of the Khevsurs, enraged, raise their shields.  
They must make Ketelauri know it;  
the bronze rings ring out sharply.  
The Khevsurs, seated in a host, are overtaken by the boys,  
one of them has seized the bones of his right hand.
